          {
            "slug": "saying-hello",
            "title": "Saying hello",
            "objective": "Greet someone in Igbo at any time of day.",
            "estMinutes": 8,
            "position": 10,
            "status": "draft",
            "body": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "In Igbo, a greeting is not small talk. It is how you acknowledge that the other person is there — and skipping it to get straight to business reads as abrupt, sometimes as rude. So this is the right place to start: before you can ask for anything, you greet."
              },
              {
                "type": "vocab"
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "text": "The greeting changes with the clock"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Igbo has no single word for hello that works at every hour. Instead you name the part of the day and add ọma, good. Get the time of day wrong and you have not made a grammar mistake — you have said something slightly absurd, like wishing someone a good night at breakfast."
              },
              {
                "type": "table",
                "title": "Greetings by time of day",
                "columns": [
                  "Time",
                  "Igbo",
                  "English"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "Morning",
                    "Ụtụtụ ọma",
                    "Good morning"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Afternoon",
                    "Ehihie ọma",
                    "Good afternoon"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Evening",
                    "Mgbede ọma",
                    "Good evening"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Night",
                    "Abalị ọma",
                    "Good night"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "grammar",
                "title": "The pattern: part of day + ọma",
                "text": "Ọma means good, and it comes after the noun it describes. So the greeting is literally morning-good, afternoon-good. This is a general Igbo pattern and worth noticing early: the describing word follows the thing described. Ụtụtụ (morning) comes first, ọma (good) second — never the other way round.",
                "examples": [
                  {
                    "igbo": "Ụtụtụ ọma",
                    "english": "Good morning — literally morning good"
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Mgbede ọma",
                    "english": "Good evening — literally evening good"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "note",
                "title": "Ndeewo works at any hour",
                "text": "If you cannot remember which greeting fits, ndeewo is safe. It is a general word of respect and acknowledgement rather than a clock-bound greeting, and it is acceptable at any time of day. Learners lean on it heavily at first, and Igbo speakers will not find that strange."
              },
              {
                "type": "phrases"
              },
              {
                "type": "dialogue",
                "title": "A morning exchange",
                "lines": [
                  {
                    "speaker": "Chidi",
                    "igbo": "Ụtụtụ ọma, nne.",
                    "english": "Good morning, mother."
                  },
                  {
                    "speaker": "Nne",
                    "igbo": "Ụtụtụ ọma, nwa m. Nnọọ.",
                    "english": "Good morning, my child. Welcome."
                  },
                  {
                    "speaker": "Chidi",
                    "igbo": "Daalụ, nne.",
                    "english": "Thank you, mother."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "tip",
                "title": "Say it out loud from the first lesson",
                "text": "Igbo is a tonal language: the same letters said on a different pitch can be a different word. That is not something you can learn by reading. Say each greeting aloud as you meet it — even quietly — because your ear needs the pitch long before your eye will notice it."
              }
