          {
            "slug": "asking-how-someone-is",
            "title": "Asking how someone is",
            "objective": "Ask how someone is doing and answer the question yourself.",
            "estMinutes": 9,
            "position": 20,
            "status": "draft",
            "body": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "A greeting opens the conversation; the next thing that happens is that somebody asks how the other is. Kèdú is the word that does it, and it is one of the most useful words in Igbo — you will hear it many times a day."
              },
              {
                "type": "vocab"
              },
              {
                "type": "grammar",
                "title": "Kèdú asks, and also means",
                "text": "Kèdú on its own is a question: how are you, how is it. Add ka (how, that) and you can ask about something specific — kèdú ka ọ dị is how are things. What is worth noticing is that kèdú is not a verb. The answer is a full statement built around dị, so you are not replying with a fixed phrase, you are making a sentence about how things are.",
                "examples": [
                  {
                    "igbo": "Kèdú?",
                    "english": "How are you?"
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Kèdú ka ọ dị?",
                    "english": "How are things?"
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Ọ dị mma.",
                    "english": "It is fine — things are good."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "text": "The verb dị: to be, in the sense of how something is"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Igbo has more than one word that English translates as to be, and they are not interchangeable. Dị describes a state or a quality — how something is. This is the one used for how-are-you. The next lesson but one introduces bụ, which is for identity, who or what something is. Getting them the wrong way round is one of the most common beginner errors, so it is worth learning them as a pair from the start."
              },
              {
