                    "Ị dị mma?",
                    "Are you fine?"
                  ],
                  [
                    "He, she, it",
                    "Ọ dị mma",
                    "He / she / it is fine"
                  ],
                  [
                    "We",
                    "Anyị dị mma",
                    "We are fine"
                  ],
                  [
                    "You (several)",
                    "Unu dị mma?",
                    "Are you all fine?"
                  ],
                  [
                    "They",
                    "Ha dị mma",
                    "They are fine"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "note",
                "title": "Igbo has two words for you",
                "text": "Ị is you when one person is being addressed. Unu is you when several are. English lost this distinction and Igbo never had any trouble with it, so a learner has to consciously choose: are you talking to one person or to a group? The pronoun is part of the verb phrase, so this is a decision you make every time you speak."
              },
