          {
            "slug": "thank-you-please-sorry",
            "title": "Thank you, please and sorry",
            "objective": "Use Igbo's three essential courtesy words, and know which apology is which.",
            "estMinutes": 9,
            "position": 30,
            "status": "draft",
            "body": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Three words carry almost all of Igbo politeness, and one of them is a trap for English speakers. Daalụ, biko and ndo look like thank you, please and sorry — and the first two are exactly that. The third is not, and using it as one is a mistake learners make for years."
              },
              {
                "type": "vocab"
              },
              {
                "type": "note",
                "title": "Ndo is sympathy, not apology",
                "text": "Ndo is what you say to someone who has stumbled, lost something, or had bad news. It means something close to I am sorry that happened to you. It is NOT what you say when you are the one at fault. If you have done something wrong, the word is gbaghara m — forgive me, excuse me. Saying ndo after stepping on someone's foot tells them you feel for their misfortune rather than that you are apologising for causing it."
              },
              {
                "type": "grammar",
                "title": "Two apologies, two jobs",
                "text": "The distinction is worth holding onto because it also shows how Igbo treats responsibility. Ndo expresses that you share someone's feeling about something that happened to them. Gbaghara m asks them to release you from something you did. The verb is gbaghara, forgive, and m is me.",
                "examples": [
                  {
                    "igbo": "Ndo.",
                    "english": "I am sorry that happened to you — sympathy."
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Gbaghara m.",
                    "english": "Forgive me, excuse me — I did this."
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Biko, gbaghara m.",
                    "english": "Please forgive me — softened, and very normal."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "text": "Turning courtesy up and down"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Daalụ and biko are complete on their own and are used constantly. To add weight, daalụ takes nke ukwuu — very much. Notice that you do not need to raise your voice or add exclamation to sound sincere; the phrase does the work."
              },
              {
                "type": "table",
                "title": "Yes and no",
                "columns": [
                  "Igbo",
                  "English",
                  "Note"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "Ee",
