          {
            "slug": "introducing-yourself",
            "title": "Introducing yourself",
            "objective": "Give your name, ask someone theirs, and say where you are from.",
            "estMinutes": 10,
            "position": 40,
            "status": "draft",
            "body": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Now that you can greet someone and ask how they are, the conversation turns to who you are. This lesson gives you the three sentences that open almost every first meeting in Igbo."
              },
              {
                "type": "vocab"
              },
              {
                "type": "grammar",
                "title": "Bụ means is, and it is not dị",
                "text": "This is the lesson where the other to be shows up. Dị, from the last lesson but one, describes how something is — a state or a quality. Bụ states what something is — an identity. Ọ dị mma means it is fine. Ọ bụ nwoke means he is a man. When you are giving your name you are stating an identity, so it is bụ every time.",
                "examples": [
                  {
                    "igbo": "Aha m bụ Chidi.",
                    "english": "My name is Chidi — identity, so bụ."
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Ọ dị mma.",
                    "english": "It is fine — state, so dị."
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Abụ m onye Igbo.",
