            "body": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Igbo leave-takings are a matched pair, and once you see the pattern it is easy to remember. One person is going and one person is staying, and each gets the wish that fits."
              },
              {
                "type": "vocab"
              },
              {
                "type": "grammar",
                "title": "Jee and nọọ — go well, stay well",
                "text": "When someone leaves, you tell them jee nke ọma — go well. When someone stays behind, you tell them nọọ nke ọma — stay well. Jee is the verb go, nọọ is stay or remain, and nke ọma means well. English collapses all of this into goodbye; Igbo says something specific about what the other person is about to do, which is part of what makes the parting warm rather than merely polite.",
                "examples": [
                  {
                    "igbo": "Jee nke ọma.",
                    "english": "Go well — said to the person leaving."
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Nọọ nke ọma.",
                    "english": "Stay well — said to the person remaining."
                  },
                  {
                    "igbo": "Ka ọ dị.",
                    "english": "Goodbye — literally let it be so."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "note",
                "title": "You say both",
                "text": "In a real parting the two are exchanged, not chosen between. The person leaving says nọọ nke ọma to the one staying, and the one staying says jee nke ọma to the one leaving. If you only remember one, jee nke ọma is the safer half, because you are usually the one going."
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "text": "Naming the next time you will meet"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Ka echi and ka anyị hụ ọzọ both defer the parting to a specific future. Ka is a versatile word meaning let or so that, and it opens all of these. Echi is tomorrow, ọzọ is again, and hụ is see."
              },
